abstract: >-
  Synthetic, dimensionless software testbench for the signed-amplitude sine
  encoding printed on p. 12 of Mun Seok Lee's preprint "Digital to Wave:
  Paradigm Shift in Quantum Wave-Based Computing and Lab-Grown Diamond
  Memory". Hash-locked v1-v5 modules implement the encoder and a matched-filter
  decoder and run seeded benchmarks for Gaussian/Laplace sample noise,
  constant carrier-phase mismatch, closed-form Gaussian calibration,
  continuous carrier-frequency offset and constant integer-sample timing
  offset, with 95% Wilson intervals, closed-form predictions and exact-offset
  oracle references. A clean-frame diagnostic shows that the timing effect is
  largely carrier-phase rotation (22.5 degrees per sample). A v6 module adds a
  blind timing-recovery baseline (matched-filter-energy window search), which in
  this easy synthetic setting matches the exact-offset oracle at every tested
  integer offset, plus fractional offsets and randomised per-frame vectors.
  A v7 module adds a joint sample-clock-drift and carrier-frequency-offset
  sweep comparing the nominal decoder, an informed oracle and a blind bounded
  joint grid search, with timing and joint acquisition reported separately.
  A v8 module diagnoses the v7 weak cell (a clock-error/CFO ridge combined
  with an interpolation-biased search metric) and adds a blind synchronizer
  with a raw-sample maximum-likelihood (GLRT) metric, coarse-to-fine
  refinement and a raw least-squares decoder, compared with the v7 blind
  search and two informed oracles on a symmetric extended grid under a
  protocol locked before the full run.
  No physical validity is claimed: nothing about
  lab-grown diamond, spin waves, phonons, hardware or biological signals is
  modelled.
